<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The February selloff from 32,800 to a trough near 30,800 coincides precisely with the outbreak of the US-Israel-Iran war on February 28; the subsequent recovery through April and May reflects the ceasefire signed April 8, partially offset by lingering energy price pressure and the fragile state of Hormuz navigation. Source: TMX Group, Trading Economics.</p>

<h2>What Shopify''s Drop Adds to the Picture</h2>

<p>Shopify''s 6.9% decline on Monday adds a third dimension to the market''s current internal logic. The company''s revenue is heavily exposed to US consumer discretionary spending, which is being compressed by energy-driven inflation. The US consumer is absorbing higher gasoline prices alongside the residual effects of tariff-driven goods price increases. Shopify does not have an energy problem directly. It has an energy-through-consumer problem, and Monday''s move suggests the market is beginning to price that transmission more aggressively.</p>

<p>For Canadian advisors, the practical question today is not whether the TSX is up or down. It is which of the two markets inside the TSX a client''s portfolio is actually exposed to. A balanced Canadian equity position holds both the mining sector''s beneficiaries and the financial sector''s casualties in the same account, and the current environment argues for a deliberate review of that balance.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who check their portfolio values daily are likely confused. The TSX appears flat to slightly positive while individual holdings are showing sharp divergence. Clients with energy or mining exposure may feel validated. Clients with bank-heavy or Shopify positions are likely unsettled, particularly those who do not understand why a "war story" is pushing financials lower rather than higher.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX headline number is not telling the whole story right now. Inside the index, we have a very clear split: gold mining names are benefiting because gold performs well in geopolitical uncertainty, and energy producers are benefiting from higher oil prices. But those same high oil prices are what''s pressuring the banks and rate-sensitive names, because elevated inflation means the Bank of Canada is less likely to cut rates this year. It''s two stories happening at the same time in the same market. What we want to do is make sure your specific portfolio is positioned for what''s actually happening, not just the headline number.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian financials, real estate investment trusts, or consumer discretionary names like Shopify. These are being repriced around the rate cut deferral.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in balanced Canadian equity funds. The mining gains and financial losses are partially offsetting, creating a deceptive sense of stability that masks real sector rotation risk.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with explicit commodity or resource equity exposure, particularly gold producers and diversified mining names.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for concentration in Canadian financials and flag for conversation if bank exposure exceeds 25% of Canadian equity allocation</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who purchased Canadian growth or tech names (including Shopify) in 2024-2025 who may be sitting on losses and need context</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check whether clients in balanced funds understand that positive headline TSX performance may be masking sector losses in their specific holdings</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all conversations about sector divergence for compliance records</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The TSX number you''re seeing doesn''t capture the full picture<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to reach out because the Canadian market is doing something unusual right now that isn''t obvious from the headline index number.<br><br>
The TSX is essentially flat to slightly positive, but that number is the result of two very different stories happening at the same time: mining and energy stocks are rising sharply, while bank stocks and rate-sensitive names are under pressure. The same oil price that is helping resource names is what''s keeping the Bank of Canada from cutting rates, which is what''s weighing on the financial sector.<br><br>
I''m reviewing our positioning to make sure we''re appropriately aligned to what''s actually happening inside the market. I''ll be in touch if I see anything that warrants a conversation, but wanted you to have context in the meantime.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors in Canadian equities:</strong> The internal sector divergence is exactly the kind of thing that catches DIY investors off guard. They see a flat TSX and feel safe, not realizing their specific holdings may be significantly underperforming.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees with bank-heavy RRSP or RRIF portfolios:</strong> Many Canadian retirees are overweight domestic bank stocks from years of dividend reinvestment. The current environment creates a meaningful conversation about whether that concentration is still appropriate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because the TSX is doing something unusual right now that I think is worth a quick conversation if you hold Canadian equities in your own accounts. The headline number looks fine, but there''s a significant split happening underneath it between resource names and financial names, and I want to make sure you understand where your portfolio sits in that picture.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>When markets post a flat or modestly positive headline number, self-directed investors tend to assume everything is fine. A professional advisor is looking past the index to the sector-level moves and asking whether a specific client''s holdings are exposed to the part of the market that''s under pressure rather than the part that''s rising.</p>
<p>The current split between Canadian mining and Canadian financials is not obvious from any standard market summary. It requires both the data and the analytical framework to interpret it. That''s exactly what a professional relationship provides.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>What does your Canadian equity exposure look like right now?</p>
<p>Do you have a sense of how much of your portfolio is in the financial sector versus the resource sector?</p>
<p>When you see the TSX up slightly on a given day, do you feel confident you understand whether your specific holdings are participating in that move?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through how the Bank of Canada''s rate decisions affect your bank stock positions?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the TSX''s flat number is hiding right now<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The TSX closed slightly higher this week, but if you hold Canadian equities, that headline number may be giving you a false sense of where things stand.<br><br>
Inside the index, mining stocks are surging while Canadian bank stocks are under real pressure, two stories moving in opposite directions at the same time, driven by the same oil price shock. Knowing which side of that split your portfolio is on matters significantly right now.<br><br>
If you''d find it useful to talk through what this means for Canadian equity holdings, I''m happy to set aside 20 minutes. No obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

