  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors managing their own fixed income or preferred share portfolios:</strong> Sustained high oil and a potential June rate increase is exactly the kind of macro shift that catches DIY investors positioned for a rate-cut environment completely off guard.</p>
<p><strong>Small business owners in energy-adjacent sectors:</strong> Fuel costs, logistics, and input price inflation from sustained $90+ oil are creating real cash flow questions that benefit from professional financial planning advice.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because the Bank of Canada has essentially said that if oil stays at current levels, they may have to raise rates in June. I know that''s the opposite of what most people were expecting this year, and I want to make sure anyone managing their own portfolio has a chance to think through what that means for their positioning.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Most investors positioned for 2026 with rate cuts as the base case. The Bank of Canada explicitly flagged that oil above $90 changes that calculus. A professional advisor is already running scenario analysis on what a June hike or hold means for each client''s specific portfolio. A self-directed investor is likely not.</p>
<p>The Hormuz situation has made macro analysis essential in a way it was not earlier this year. That is exactly when a professional relationship delivers its most visible value.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
