VALUES (
  '2026/05/12/boc-inflation-april-cpi-june-decision',
  'economy', 'article',
  'The Bank of Canada Is Holding the Line. But the Line Is Moving.',
  'CPI inflation rose to 2.4% in March on the largest monthly gasoline price increase on record, and the BoC projected it will likely peak near 3% in April. Statistics Canada releases April CPI on May 19. Whatever that number shows will determine whether Macklem''s rate hike language remains hypothetical or becomes operative.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada held its policy rate at 2.25% on April 29,</strong><span> the fourth consecutive hold, as Governing Council chose to look through the immediate inflation impact of higher energy prices while monitoring for second-round effects.</span></li>
<li><strong>CPI inflation rose to 2.4% in March,</strong><span> up from 1.8% in February, driven by the largest monthly gasoline price increase on record. Core inflation measures held steady at just above 2%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The BoC projected inflation will likely peak near 3% in April</strong><span> before easing back toward 2% by early 2027, a forecast that explicitly assumes oil prices moderate. With Brent at $94 this week, that assumption is under stress.</span></li>
<li><strong>April CPI is released by Statistics Canada on May 19.</strong><span> If the number comes in at or above 3%, it will materially change the calculus for the June 10 decision.</span></li>
<li><strong>Governor Macklem stated that if oil prices remain elevated,</strong><span> "there may be a need for consecutive increases in the policy rate," unusually direct language that marks a genuine pivot in BoC communication tone.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Bank of Canada has now held its policy rate at 2.25% for four consecutive decisions. That consistency is not stubbornness. It is the product of a deliberate analytical framework: Governing Council decided in April to look through the immediate inflationary impact of higher energy prices on the grounds that a supply shock does not warrant a policy tightening response unless it feeds into persistent broad-based price increases. The question in front of advisors today is whether that framework survives the May 19 CPI release.</p>

<p>The March CPI number set the table. Inflation rose from 1.8% in February to 2.4% in March, driven primarily by gasoline. Statistics Canada described the gasoline component as the largest monthly increase on record, a direct consequence of the Hormuz disruption. Core inflation measures, which the Bank uses as its operational guide, told a more contained story: CPI-trim, CPI-median, and CPI-common all held steady just above 2% in March. The BoC took those core readings as evidence that the energy shock had not yet fed into broader goods and services prices. "So far, there is little evidence that higher oil prices have fed through to other goods and services prices more broadly," Macklem said on April 29. "But it is early days."</p>

<h2>What April CPI Will Tell the Bank</h2>

<p>The April CPI release, scheduled for May 19, will be the first test of that "early days" framework. The Bank''s own projection has total CPI peaking near 3% in April. If April CPI comes in at 3% or above, it will confirm the Bank''s forecast and maintain the hold scenario. If it comes in materially above 3%, particularly if core measures show any upward movement, the calculus for June 10 shifts meaningfully.</p>

<p>The Bank''s April baseline forecast assumes Brent crude will gradually decline from US$90 per barrel in Q2 to US$75 by mid-2027. With Brent currently above $94 and the ceasefire under strain, that assumption is already failing its first real test. National Bank of Canada economists expect the policy rate to remain at 2.25% through 2026 and then rise to 2.50% in Q1 2027. But that forecast was built before the ceasefire fragility became apparent this week.</p>

<p>The chart above shows Canadian CPI headline and core measures over the past fourteen months alongside the Bank of Canada policy rate, illustrating the divergence that has opened between energy-driven headline inflation and the core measures that drive actual BoC decisions.</p>

