<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Brent crude has retraced approximately 21% from its $120 peak following the April 8 ceasefire, while the VIX, a measure of implied market volatility, remains elevated relative to the pre-war baseline. The divergence between oil''s partial recovery and persistent investor anxiety is consistent with the availability heuristic delaying cognitive adjustment after a salient negative event. Source: Trading Economics, Yahoo Finance.</p>

<h2>What Thaler''s Research Adds to the Picture</h2>

<p>Richard Thaler''s work on mental accounting offers a complementary lens. In the current environment, investors tend to mentally segregate their energy-related holdings from the rest of their portfolio, treating the oil shock as a distinct category of risk rather than analysing its actual net effect on their specific portfolio. A client with a balanced Canadian portfolio has exposure to energy stocks that benefit from high oil and bank stocks that are hurt by it, in roughly offsetting proportions. The availability heuristic causes the oil shock to feel like a pure negative because the negative news is what dominates the information environment. The positive effects, energy sector gains, do not generate the same media coverage. Thaler would recognise this as a classic mental accounting failure: the threat is vivid, the offset is invisible.</p>

<p>The advisory implication is concrete. Clients who are currently feeling that their portfolios are heavily damaged by the oil shock may be surprised to learn the actual numbers, which in many balanced accounts reflect a more complicated and less alarming picture. The conversation that helps a client see the full ledger rather than the available portion of it is exactly the kind of cognitive intervention that Kahneman and Thaler''s research identifies as the advisor''s highest-value function.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who consume financial news regularly are in a state of heightened anxiety that is not fully calibrated to their actual portfolio positions. The vivid, dramatic coverage of the Hormuz crisis has made the worst-case scenario feel like the current reality. Many are fighting the impulse to reduce risk in a way that would lock in losses.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">What you''re feeling right now is a very normal response to a lot of alarming news coverage. Here''s what I want to share with you: the research on investor behaviour is actually very consistent on what happens when people make portfolio decisions based on news intensity. They tend to sell after the damage has already happened, and they tend to buy back in after the recovery is already well underway. Brent crude actually fell 21% from its peak in March after the ceasefire in April, which you probably didn''t hear as loudly as the news about it hitting $120. The oil story is genuinely uncertain right now, and I''m not going to pretend otherwise. But the question I want to work through with you is: what does your actual portfolio look like, and does the action you''re feeling like taking actually improve your position based on the real numbers, rather than based on what feels available right now?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who are news-heavy information consumers and who have messaged or called about making defensive portfolio changes in the past eight weeks. These are the availability heuristic''s primary subjects.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients who have not called but whose social media and conversation patterns suggest anxiety. A proactive check-in is warranted.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who have done nothing and need reassurance that inaction in this environment is often the correct response, backed by research.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the list of clients who have initiated contact since February 28 expressing anxiety or requesting defensive changes; prioritise follow-up</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For any client who executed a defensive portfolio change in March or April, schedule a review to assess whether the decision reflected the actual portfolio picture or the news environment</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-page "actual versus perceived" document showing the real net portfolio effect of the oil shock for a representative balanced client</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all behavioural conversations with clients for compliance records</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the oil news is making feel worse than it is<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I know the coverage of the Iran conflict and oil prices has been intense and alarming over the past few months. I wanted to share a data point that often gets lost in that coverage: Brent crude has already fallen about 21% from its peak in late March, after the ceasefire in April provided partial relief. The worst-case scenario that dominated headlines in March has not, so far, materialised at full severity.<br><br>
None of that means the situation is resolved. It genuinely isn''t. But the research on how investors make decisions in high-news environments is consistent and clear: decisions made based on news intensity rather than actual portfolio data tend to be poorly timed.<br><br>
When we look at your actual numbers, I want to walk you through what the net effect of the last ten weeks has been on your portfolio specifically, not what the headlines suggest. I think you may be surprised by what the real picture looks like.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors in balanced or conservative portfolios:</strong> Self-directed investors who have been making reactive changes in response to the oil shock news cycle are exactly the Barber-Odean population. They have no one to help them distinguish between news intensity and actual portfolio risk.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees drawing from non-registered accounts:</strong> This group faces the most direct harm from making emotionally driven asset allocation changes, as crystallised losses in drawdown portfolios are particularly difficult to recover.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m calling because the research on investor behaviour in high-news environments like the current one is very consistent about what happens when people make portfolio changes based on news intensity rather than their actual numbers. I want to offer you a conversation that focuses on your real portfolio data rather than the news cycle, because those are often two very different pictures right now.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The availability heuristic is a known, documented cognitive pattern that systematically produces poor investment timing in exactly the conditions that currently exist in Canadian markets. An advisor who understands behavioral finance can show a client what their actual portfolio picture looks like, separate from the news environment, and can provide the cognitive counterweight that prevents the most costly reactive decisions.</p>
<p>Barber and Odean''s research found 2.65 percentage points of annual underperformance from attention-driven trading. That is not a small number over a decade of retirement savings.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How much time have you been spending following the oil price news over the past few months?</p>
<p>Have you made any changes to your portfolio in response to the news about Iran and oil prices?</p>
<p>When you look at your portfolio right now, do you know what the actual net effect of the last two months has been on your specific holdings?</p>
<p>Do you have someone you talk to when the news makes you feel like you should do something with your investments?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The research on investor decisions in high-news environments<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
There''s a well-documented pattern in behavioral finance research: when news coverage of a financial event is dramatic and intense, investors make portfolio decisions based on what feels most salient rather than what their actual numbers show. The result is systematically poor timing.<br><br>
Barber and Odean''s research on 66,000 household accounts found that attention-driven trading underperforms buy-and-hold by 2.65 percentage points annually. In the current environment, with oil prices having already recovered 21% from their March peak without much notice, that pattern is worth thinking about.<br><br>
If you manage your own portfolio and would find it useful to talk through what your actual exposure looks like versus what the news suggests, I''m available for a 20-minute conversation this week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
